    slug: "parents-guide-to-supporting-a-netball-athlete",
    title:
      "Netball Parents: How to Support Your Child's Development (and What Quietly Holds Them Back)",
    excerpt:
      "Former Diamond Caitlyn Strachan on how netball parents can support a developing athlete, what quietly hurts, and the questions to ask after a game.",
    date: "2026-05-04",
    updated: "2026-10-28",
    readMinutes: 5,
    category: "Mindset",
    body: [
      "Most netball parents I meet are doing far more right than they realise. They drive to training in the rain, pack the snacks, learn the rules and cheer hard on Saturday mornings. The challenge is keeping that support strong without letting it tip into pressure, because pressure is what quietly takes the love of the game away.",
      "After coaching hundreds of athletes from Net Set Go through to senior representative level, here's what I've seen work and what I've seen go wrong.",
      "## What genuinely helps a young netballer",
      "**Be a great driver, not a great coach.** Your job in the car after the game is to listen, not to break down the third quarter. Ask one open question, like \"Did you have fun?\", and leave the analysis to the coach. The athletes who go furthest are the ones whose parents make the car ride feel safe after a loss, not just after a win.",
      "**Watch the effort, not the scoreboard.** Every elite player I know remembers the parents who praised the work, not the result. Try \"I loved how hard you chased that ball\" or \"You looked really focused today.\" For a developing athlete, that's gold.",
      "**Back the coach, even when you disagree.** A young player can hold one set of instructions in their head during a game. If they hear something different from the sideline, the whole structure falls apart. Disagree privately, support publicly.",
      "## What quietly hurts, even with the best intentions",
      "**Comparing your child to others.** Especially out loud, and especially to their face. Two athletes developing at completely different rates can both end up in elite programs. Comparison only teaches your child that someone else is the standard. They aren't.",
      "**Coaching from the sideline.** Yelling \"pass it\", \"shoot\" or \"mark her\" forces your child to listen to two voices at once. It also tells them you don't trust them to make the call. Both are confidence killers.",
      "**Conversations that are only about outcomes.** If every chat after netball is about whether they won, how many they scored or whether they made the team, netball eventually stops being theirs. It becomes yours.",
      "## Better questions to ask after a game",
      "Instead of \"Did you win?\" or \"How many did you score?\", try:",
      "- What did you enjoy about today?\n- What's something you tried that you've been working on?\n- Is there a teammate I should say well done to?",
      "These questions teach your child to judge their performance against their own goals rather than the scoreboard. In my experience, that habit is what separates the players who plateau from the ones who keep climbing.",
      "## The long view: why patience wins in netball",
      "Athletes peak at very different ages. Early developers often peak first and then plateau, while late bloomers (myself included) often catch up and pass them. Whichever camp your child is in, your job is the same: keep the love alive, keep them around the game and trust the process.",
      "Premier league, state teams and the Diamonds are real goals, but they're decided by what happens between 17 and 23, not 11 and 12. Patience is the most underrated parenting skill in netball.",
      "Related reading: if your child is heading into trials, read Trials Aren't Just About Talent for what selectors really watch and how to support them on the day.",
      "Want to give your child more confidence on court? One-on-one and small group sessions with CC Netball build the fundamentals and the belief to back them. Make an enquiry at ccnetball.com.",
      "Written by Caitlyn Strachan",
      "Caitlyn Strachan is a former Australian Diamond, triple ANZ Championship premiership player and founder of CC Netball.",
    ],
